                <pb facs="0550" n="436" sameAs="frus:frus1924v01/pg436"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.01/147: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Mayer</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-12-06T17:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">December 6,
                                1924—5 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received December 6—2:11
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>474. Your 302, December 5, 2 p.m., second paragraph, and last sentence paragraph
                    8 my 472, December 4, 3 p.m.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>British Minister has likewise received authority to sign proposed
                        statement. His Government has informed him that they are urging French
                        Government to join in proposed action. He is further authorized to sign even
                        without French concurrence if I agree.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>While I consider far preferable that all Powers concerned should join in
                        proposed action, I respectfully request authority to sign without French
                        concurrence if representative here not instructed to sign by the time
                        Department’s reply to this telegram received.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>Effectiveness of the proposed action would be greatly impaired if not
                        destroyed, it is submitted, should statement not be transmitted to Chinese
                        Government and published prior to any hostile declaration by Dr. Sun Yat-sen
                        advocating immediate cancelation or revision of so-called “unjust treaties”.
                        See last sentence paragraph 6 my 472 mentioned above.</item>
                </list>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Mayer</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
